Apply the final logit soft cap (and its pre-scale) in the Transformers backend

transformers applies MuseGlimmer's logit transform in
`MuseGlimmerForConditionalGeneration.forward`:

    logits = logits * config.output_multiplier      # 0.19611613513818404
    logits = final_logit_softcapping * tanh(logits / final_logit_softcapping)

That is one level above `self.model(...)`, which is all the Transformers
backend runs. The backend then computes its own logits, reading a `logit_scale`
attribute (MuseGlimmer spells it `output_multiplier`, so it defaults to 1.0)
and never passing `soft_cap` at all. Neither transform survives.

Both are monotonic, so greedy decoding is unaffected -- which is why this hides
behind the embedding bug rather than causing an obvious failure of its own. What
it corrupts is every logprob the engine reports and every temperature-dependent
sampling decision: the effective temperature is 1/0.196 ~= 5.1x too low. A
top-1 reported at -0.0025 is really about -0.27.

The two cannot simply both be passed to LogitsProcessor, which applies its
`scale` *after* the cap; MuseGlimmer needs it before. Gemma 2 never exercised
the combination because it caps without pre-scaling. But the existing
LogitsProcessor can express it exactly, by folding the multiplier into the cap:

    tanh(z / (T/m)) * (T/m) * m  ==  T * tanh(z * m / T)

so `soft_cap = T/m` with `scale = m` is the intended function, and reduces to
the plain cap at m == 1. No change to LogitsProcessor is needed.

Verified on `turboderp/Muse-Glimmer-30B-exl3` @2.00bpw against native
exllamav3, which models the same pre-scale-then-softcap: with this and the
embedding-postprocess patch, 40 greedy tokens match token for token, and the
step-0 top-15 logprobs agree to ~0.03 nats -- the fp16 accumulation noise floor
already characterized for this backend.

Not EXL3-specific, and not Muse-Glimmer-specific either: vaultgemma, nanochat,
t5gemma and t5gemma2 all carry final_logit_softcapping and have no native vLLM
implementation, so the backend is the only way to serve them and it drops the cap.

Known gap: the Granite family spells its scale `logits_scaling` and divides rather
than multiplies. Not covered here -- no Granite checkpoint was on hand to verify
against, and guessing at an inverse is how the bug being fixed got written.

